# The slack/share pairs that can be compared at all. One row per (layer, buffer, holder kind)
# where both the slack and the share state a unit.
#
# Both sides must state a unit
#   A slack is compared against holder shares, so it has to be in the same unit, and a pair where
#   either side declines to say is not a mismatch, it is an unanswerable question. Making the
#   population "both stated" rather than "all pairs" is the difference between a rule that examines
#   something and a rule that reports on rows it cannot read.
#
# The absorbing buffer's slack only
#   It is the absorbing buffer's slack, not every slack in the layer, and that narrowing is the
#   point of the file. Join entries/slacks.sqlc on (filing, layer) alone and every holder is
#   compared against all three of its layer's buffers. Most of those pairs are a holder against a
#   buffer that absorbs nothing of theirs, where a differing unit is no defect at all, and
#   checks/slack_unit_mismatch.sqlc, whose sentence is 'the unit of the shares it bounds', would
#   report all of them as its examined population.
#
# Every holder, not only the ones the slack bounds
#   The slack bounds the served holders' shares, and a unit rule could stop at them. It does not,
#   because a remainder's shares are a distribution of `|r|` and are all in the layer's unit
#   whichever holder carries them, as is the slack. A slack and a share that disagree are a defect
#   whether the share was served or went without, and the unserved holders are often the only
#   ones a layer states, as `enterprise-contract`'s `capability` does.
#
# The guard covers its site, and the extra rows are unit checks
#   arithmetic/roster.sqlc names this rule the guard for `shares_bounded`, and that site reads the
#   absorbing buffer against the served holders under interference. On the buffer the guard is no
#   wider than the site: a holder against a buffer that absorbs nothing of theirs is no defect, and
#   counting it would be a guard answering a different question. On the holders it is wider on
#   purpose, for the reason above, so every pair the site compares is checked here and the rest are
#   checks of the unit claim alone, which reports/coverage.sqlc counts as this rule's own.
-- entries/absorbing_slack.sqlc against pm:Remainder/pm:holder, each carrying its own unit.
SELECT s.filing, s.layer, s.buffer, h.kind,
       s.unit AS slack_unit, h.share_unit
FROM      (
    :compose(entries/absorbing_slack.sqlc)
) s
JOIN      (
    :compose(entries/holders.sqlc)
) h USING (filing, layer)
WHERE s.unit IS NOT NULL AND h.share_unit IS NOT NULL
